-- Backfill missing instruction cards + import new LR guided drills (from LSAT Lab source).
-- Generated from data/guided-drills/_seed.json. Idempotent.

-- 1) Backfill cards on existing drills (match by title; only updates instruction_card / question_count).
UPDATE public.guided_drills SET instruction_card=$gd${"left_md":"## This set has 7 **Principle-Strengthen**\n## questions from tests 55-59\n\nAll of these examples are the much-more common style of Principle-Strengthen in which the _answers_ are principles. They often feel more like **Sufficient Assumption** than Strengthen, because the correct answer is usually a conditional rule that is triggered by the Evidence and results in the Conclusion.\n\nBut the question stem is technically a Strengthen task (which answer _most_ justifies), so we're not supposed to ask ourselves, \"Does this answer prove the conclusion\", even though in many cases it will. In some rare cases, though, the correct answer could be as weakly worded as a Necessary Assumption.\n\nNevertheless, our primary approach to these is to expect a conditional logic answer, so we pre-phrase by articulating an \"IF premise, THEN conclusion\" reasoning move.\n\nMost of the answers will be conditionals. If so, we want to be very **stubborn with the outcome** . The right side of the rule should match the conclusion (or at least sound like wording that would strongly support the conclusion). We want to be **flexible with the trigger** .\n\nOther answers feel more like comparative ideas that help _Weigh Tradeoffs_ or establish priorities. When arguments seem to be balancing competing ideas (a pro and a con / this obligation with that obligation), then when the author issues her judgment in the conclusion, she's revealing whether she cares more about the good thing or the bad thing, whether she cares more about this obligation or that obligation.\n\nGiven this argument, we'd anticipate a conditional answer\n_Billy lied to our customers. Thus he shouldn't be on our staff anymore._\n\n(A) A person can be on the staff only if they are consistently truthful with customers\n\nGiven this argument, we might see a weighing-tradeoffs answer\n_Billy is extremely dependable, although she lied to our customers. Clearly, she shouldn't be on our staff._\n\n(A) Dependability is a less important trait in a staff member than is honesty.","right_md":"## Try these 7 problems, **untimed**\n\n- Once you find the Conclusion and identify the main Supporting Idea(s), you will generally want to articulate an, \"If [premise], then [conclusion]\" linking idea.\n\n- If, otherwise, you felt like the argument was grappling with competing interests, then articulate how the author is weighing tradeoffs:  \"she cares more about X than about Y\".\n\n- Try to get rid of a lot answers quickly because they don't have any good matching language for the conclusion, or because the matching language for the conclusion is being put on the **left** of the conditional arrow.\n\n- Correct answers will often, if not usually, be written in contrapositive form, so be willing to try that move. If you read \"should be on staff only if consistently truthful\", then you have to make your brain say, \"Okay, this is saying _if you're_ **_not_** _consistently truthful, should_ **_not_** _on staff_ \"."}$gd$::jsonb WHERE title=$gd$Principle-Strengthen (set 2)$gd$;
UPDATE public.guided_drills SET instruction_card=$gd${"left_md":"Find the gap between a Rule and its Application\non these five **Principle-Strengthen** questions\n\n_example_\nPRINCIPLE: Lies are justified if they do more good than harm.\nAPPLICATION: Jason falsely claimed that Sharon has a driver's license to spare her getting mocked by her friends. Since he did manage to spare her the mockery, he was justified in lying.\n\nIn order to use this Rule to prove that a lie was justified, we have to establish the trigger:\n_the lie did more good than harm_\n\nSo the correct answer would need to convince us that \"keeping Sharon from getting mocked = did more good than harm\".\n\n(Note: there are two styles of Principle-Strengthen question:  _Principles in Stimulus vs. Principle in Answers_ . The latter kind is more than 10x more common, but the former kind shows up more on modern tests than it used to)","right_md":"- Think about each Principle as having some criteria that must be met to trigger the rule, and having an outcome that matches up with the Application's conclusion.\n\n- The Application will (almost?) always conclude the right side of the Principle.\n\n- What we're being tested on is how the Evidence in the Application paragraph failed to clearly trigger the legal definition of the Principle.\n\n- Think about which part(s) of the Principle's trigger wasn't clearly established, and keep that mantra in your head as you consider answer choices."}$gd$::jsonb WHERE title=$gd$Principle-Strengthen + Application (Set 1)$gd$;
UPDATE public.guided_drills SET instruction_card=$gd${"left_md":"This set contains **four RC passages** , each one with a different Framework.\n\nThere is an Old / New, a Problem / Solution, a Present Debate, and a Challenge Position, along with the Main Point or Primary Purpose question accompanying that passage.\n\nRead each passage, trying to discern the Framework and reading for the Framework Fill-In's. Think about the main point through the lens of the author's purpose. _Why_ is the author writing about this topic? If she could have us read only one sentence, which one would best capture her purpose in writing the passage?","right_md":"## Attempt these four passages\n## and the accompanying questions, **untimed.**"}$gd$::jsonb WHERE title=$gd$Framework Sampler$gd$;

-- 2) Insert new LR drills (upsert by slug).
INSERT INTO public.guided_drills (slug,week,order_in_week,title,category,estimated_minutes,question_count,difficulty_min,difficulty_max,question_types,question_features,reasoning_types,start_button_label,instruction_card,bank_filter,source_file,is_published) VALUES ($gd$w10-assumption-family-medium-set-2$gd$,10,6,$gd$Assumption Family (Medium) Set 2$gd$,$gd$Process$gd$,40,10,3,NULL,ARRAY[$gd$Sufficient Assumption$gd$,$gd$Necessary Assumption$gd$,$gd$Principle-Strengthen$gd$,$gd$Strengthen$gd$,$gd$Weaken$gd$,$gd$Flaw$gd$,$gd$Evaluate$gd$]::text[],ARRAY[$gd$Three Main Reasoning Structures$gd$]::text[],ARRAY[$gd$Conditional Logic$gd$,$gd$Comparisons$gd$,$gd$Causation$gd$]::text[],$gd$Start$gd$,$gd${"left_md":"All question types in the Assumption Family will sometimes test a mathematical **conditional** **logic** understanding and other times test more \"shades of gray\" thinking like **comparisons** and **causal** explanations. But different question types do have strong tendencies. The graphic below shows an approximate continuum. We expect more formal logical thinking towards the top and more fuzzy real-world conversation towards the bottom.\n**Internal Logic**\nmissing links                 **Sufficient Assumption**\nmissing facts\nrules, definitions             **Principle**\nand vs. or                   (strengthen / conform)\nconditional logic\ntrigger = evidence\noutcome = conclusion          **Necessary Assumption**\nruling out all alternatives\n**Famous Flaws** **Flaw / Parallel Flaw**\n**Courtroom Debate**\nadvantages, disadvantages\nrebuttals\nrecommendations             **Strengthen**\npredictions                 **Weaken**\nhypotheses                **Evaluate**\nsurveys\nexperiments\nstatistics","right_md":"## **Try these 10 problems, untimed.**\n\nThink about shifting your thinking back and forth between different reasoning structures\n\n**conditional** thinking\n- rules, definitions, trigger, outcome, contrapositive\n\n**causal** thinking\n- curious fact / author's explanation\n- alternate explanations (reverse cause / 3rd factor)\n- plausibility of author's explanation (no cause, no effect / effect without cause / cause without effect)\n\n**comparison** thinking\n- pros vs. cons  /  weighing tradeoffs\n- current world vs.  Plan/Prediction world\n- then vs. now  /  now vs. the future\n- this group vs. that group\n- sample vs. general population\n- part vs. whole\n- that situation vs. this situation\n\nAnd when you're doing **Flaw** , also consider the 10 **Famous Flaws.**"}$gd$::jsonb,$gd${"qtypes":["Sufficient Assumption","Necessary Assumption","Principle-Strengthen","Strengthen","Weaken","Flaw","Evaluate"],"features":["Three Main Reasoning Structures"],"reasoning_types":["Conditional Logic","Comparisons","Causation"],"difficulty_min":3,"difficulty_max":null,"count":10}$gd$::jsonb,$gd$lsatlab-studyplan$gd$,true)
ON CONFLICT (slug) DO UPDATE SET instruction_card=EXCLUDED.instruction_card, bank_filter=EXCLUDED.bank_filter, question_count=EXCLUDED.question_count, estimated_minutes=EXCLUDED.estimated_minutes, difficulty_min=EXCLUDED.difficulty_min, difficulty_max=EXCLUDED.difficulty_max, updated_at=now();

INSERT INTO public.guided_drills (slug,week,order_in_week,title,category,estimated_minutes,question_count,difficulty_min,difficulty_max,question_types,question_features,reasoning_types,start_button_label,instruction_card,bank_filter,source_file,is_published) VALUES ($gd$w11-explain-curious-fact-easier-set-2$gd$,11,5,$gd$Explain Curious Fact (Easier) Set 2$gd$,$gd$Pacing$gd$,25,6,1,2,ARRAY[$gd$Explain Curious Fact$gd$]::text[],ARRAY[$gd$Explain Curious Fact$gd$]::text[],ARRAY[$gd$Causation$gd$]::text[],$gd$Start$gd$,$gd${"left_md":"These six questions each involve a \"curious\" background fact, and at least one potential explanation offered for that fact. We call this pattern **Explain Curious Fact**\n\nThere are a handful of different question types here, but they all have a common theme:\n- The **evidence** presents some **Curious Fact** (an asymmetric comparison like a correlation, a statistical change, or a difference between two groups or situations)\n\n- The author's **conclusion** will state or assume one possible **Causal Explanation** for this curious fact.\n\n- The correct answer will either deal with **Alternate Explanations** for the curious fact or with the **plausibility** **of the Author's Explanation** .","right_md":"## **Try these 6 questions in 10 minutes**\n(1.5x = 15 mins, 2x = 20 mins)\n\nWhat is the **Curious Fact** ?****\n- what language tips us off?  is there an _Asymmetric Comparison_ ?\n- _ask_ yourself the Curious Fact:  \"Why is it that ... [curious fact]?\"\n\nWhat is the **Author's Explanation / Hypothesis** _?_\n- Is there a verb or expression in the conclusion indicating that the author is making a causal claim?\n- Make sure that you spell out every step in the author's causal story. It's very common for an author to fail to mention each step in the causal chain that gets them from their causal explanation to the actual curious fact.\n\nDoes the correct answer deal with an **Alternate Explanation** ?\n- Is it dealing with the idea of _Reverse Causality_ ?\n- Is it dealing with the idea of a _Third Factor_ ?\n\nOr does it address the **plausibility** of the Author's Explanation?\n- Is it addressing plausibility via covariation? (strengthening by _No Cause / No Effect_ , or _More Cause / More Effect_ data points or weakening by _Cause without Effect_ , or _Effect without Cause_ data points)\n- Or is it addressing plausibility some other way?  (placing the suspect at the scene of the crime, or explaining the motive/mechanism by which the crime would have taken place)"}$gd$::jsonb,$gd${"qtypes":["Explain Curious Fact"],"features":["Explain Curious Fact"],"reasoning_types":["Causation"],"difficulty_min":1,"difficulty_max":2,"count":6}$gd$::jsonb,$gd$lsatlab-studyplan$gd$,true)
ON CONFLICT (slug) DO UPDATE SET instruction_card=EXCLUDED.instruction_card, bank_filter=EXCLUDED.bank_filter, question_count=EXCLUDED.question_count, estimated_minutes=EXCLUDED.estimated_minutes, difficulty_min=EXCLUDED.difficulty_min, difficulty_max=EXCLUDED.difficulty_max, updated_at=now();

INSERT INTO public.guided_drills (slug,week,order_in_week,title,category,estimated_minutes,question_count,difficulty_min,difficulty_max,question_types,question_features,reasoning_types,start_button_label,instruction_card,bank_filter,source_file,is_published) VALUES ($gd$w10-famous-flaws-easier$gd$,10,7,$gd$Famous Flaws (Easier)$gd$,$gd$Process$gd$,25,5,1,2,ARRAY[$gd$Flaw$gd$]::text[],ARRAY[$gd$Famous Flaws$gd$]::text[],ARRAY[]::text[],$gd$Start$gd$,$gd${"left_md":"## **Try 8** **Famous Flaw** **problems**\n**tests:** 127, 129, 130 / **levels:** 1-3\n\nGet better at spotting some famous flaw moves in the argument, and better at spotting some \"decoy\" famous flaws appearing in wrong answer choices.\n\nMany of the answers are not describing famous flaws. Some of the answer choices that are describing famous flaws will sound like the \"classic\" abstract language definition of that flaw, while others will use more unique or specific language to allude to the famous flaw.","right_md":"## Attempt these 8 questions **untimed** , for accuracy\n\nHere's a quick basketball related example for each of the ten main famous flaws.\n\nHere's what to ask yourself if you see an answer choice describing one of these flaws:"}$gd$::jsonb,$gd${"qtypes":["Flaw"],"features":["Famous Flaws"],"reasoning_types":[],"difficulty_min":1,"difficulty_max":2,"count":5}$gd$::jsonb,$gd$lsatlab-studyplan$gd$,true)
ON CONFLICT (slug) DO UPDATE SET instruction_card=EXCLUDED.instruction_card, bank_filter=EXCLUDED.bank_filter, question_count=EXCLUDED.question_count, estimated_minutes=EXCLUDED.estimated_minutes, difficulty_min=EXCLUDED.difficulty_min, difficulty_max=EXCLUDED.difficulty_max, updated_at=now();

INSERT INTO public.guided_drills (slug,week,order_in_week,title,category,estimated_minutes,question_count,difficulty_min,difficulty_max,question_types,question_features,reasoning_types,start_button_label,instruction_card,bank_filter,source_file,is_published) VALUES ($gd$w10-famous-flaws-easier-set-2$gd$,10,8,$gd$Famous Flaws (Easier) Set 2$gd$,$gd$Pacing$gd$,30,5,1,2,ARRAY[$gd$Flaw$gd$]::text[],ARRAY[$gd$Famous Flaws$gd$]::text[],ARRAY[]::text[],$gd$Start$gd$,$gd${"left_md":"## Try 9**** **Famous Flaw** **** problems\n**tests:** 60-64 / **levels:** 1-3\n\nGet better at spotting some famous flaw moves in the argument, and better at spotting some \"decoy\" famous flaws appearing in wrong answer choices.\n\nMany of the answers are not describing famous flaws. Some of the answer choices that are describing famous flaws will sound like the \"classic\" abstract language definition of that flaw, while others will use more unique or specific language to allude to the famous flaw.","right_md":"- Set a timer for **14 mins** (1.5x = 21 mins, 2x = 28 mins)\n\n- Try to get an answer to all 12 of these problems.\n\n- Select your answer, but don't hit Confirm.\n\n- Flag any of them that feel like a struggle or a low-confidence guess.\n\n- When the timer goes off, flip back through them and revisit your thinking in an untimed way, before then hitting Confirm on your final answer.\n\n- Check answers and explanations."}$gd$::jsonb,$gd${"qtypes":["Flaw"],"features":["Famous Flaws"],"reasoning_types":[],"difficulty_min":1,"difficulty_max":2,"count":5}$gd$::jsonb,$gd$lsatlab-studyplan$gd$,true)
ON CONFLICT (slug) DO UPDATE SET instruction_card=EXCLUDED.instruction_card, bank_filter=EXCLUDED.bank_filter, question_count=EXCLUDED.question_count, estimated_minutes=EXCLUDED.estimated_minutes, difficulty_min=EXCLUDED.difficulty_min, difficulty_max=EXCLUDED.difficulty_max, updated_at=now();

INSERT INTO public.guided_drills (slug,week,order_in_week,title,category,estimated_minutes,question_count,difficulty_min,difficulty_max,question_types,question_features,reasoning_types,start_button_label,instruction_card,bank_filter,source_file,is_published) VALUES ($gd$w12-missing-links-set-2$gd$,12,5,$gd$Missing Links Set 2$gd$,$gd$Process$gd$,30,6,NULL,NULL,ARRAY[$gd$Sufficient Assumption$gd$,$gd$Necessary Assumption$gd$,$gd$Principle-Strengthen$gd$]::text[],ARRAY[$gd$Missing Links$gd$]::text[],ARRAY[]::text[],$gd$Start$gd$,$gd${"left_md":"## These 8 questions test our ability to figure out\n## which fact or link is missing in an argument.\n\nSome of them involve arguments in which there's a _New Concept_ that appears in the conclusion, which means we need to link that new concept to whatever it's being associated with in the evidence.\n\nSome of them involve an attempt to apply a principle such as, \"If A and B are true, then X is true\". The argument ends up concluding X, but it failed to establish all the ingredients in the trigger (in this case, A and B).","right_md":"Try to predict what's missing. Sometimes, your intuition will clearly hear something missing. Other times, you'll need to ask yourself questions in order to find it.\n\n- Which claim is the Conclusion?\n- Is there any wording appearing in the conclusion that never shows up in the evidence?\n- If so, what concept in the evidence is that _New Wording in the Conclusion_ supposed to match up with?\n- Are there any conditional rules or principles provided? If so has the author clearly established that the trigger is true?\n\nAs you review these problems,\n**correct answers -**\ndoes it supply a Missing Link? Was it the contrapositive of your prediction? did it address a Missing Fact that we need to trigger a conditional rule or principle?\n\n**incorrect answers -**\ndoes it have the **Wrong Strength** ?\ndoes it lack some **Needed Idea** (such as a New Idea in the conclusion)\nis it **Irrelevant** for some reason?\nit is an illegal **Reversal / Negation** of the connection we wanted?"}$gd$::jsonb,$gd${"qtypes":["Sufficient Assumption","Necessary Assumption","Principle-Strengthen"],"features":["Missing Links"],"reasoning_types":[],"difficulty_min":null,"difficulty_max":null,"count":6}$gd$::jsonb,$gd$lsatlab-studyplan$gd$,true)
ON CONFLICT (slug) DO UPDATE SET instruction_card=EXCLUDED.instruction_card, bank_filter=EXCLUDED.bank_filter, question_count=EXCLUDED.question_count, estimated_minutes=EXCLUDED.estimated_minutes, difficulty_min=EXCLUDED.difficulty_min, difficulty_max=EXCLUDED.difficulty_max, updated_at=now();

INSERT INTO public.guided_drills (slug,week,order_in_week,title,category,estimated_minutes,question_count,difficulty_min,difficulty_max,question_types,question_features,reasoning_types,start_button_label,instruction_card,bank_filter,source_file,is_published) VALUES ($gd$w13-describing-arguments-match-family$gd$,13,1,$gd$Describing Arguments (Match Family)$gd$,$gd$Process$gd$,35,10,NULL,NULL,ARRAY[$gd$Role$gd$,$gd$Method$gd$,$gd$Main Conclusion$gd$]::text[],ARRAY[$gd$Describing Arguments$gd$]::text[],ARRAY[]::text[],$gd$Start$gd$,$gd${"left_md":"This 10 question set is comprised of three questions from the Match Family, which test our ability to understand what role certain claims are playing:  Role, Method, and Main Conclusion.\n\n## **Role** and **Main Conclusion**\n\nMost questions \"hide\" the conclusion in one of two ways:\n- it's the **very first claim** , or\n- it's attached to some **pivot / rebuttal**\n\n**Opinion** indicators and **Pivot** indicators are very helpful for finding the parts of the argument.\n\nWhen we're assessing whether something is a conclusion (be it main conclusion or intermediate conclusion), we should ask ourselves,\n\n\"**Is this Supported** ? Why should we believe this?\"\nIf no support it provided, then it isn't a conclusion.\n\n## **Method**\n\nThese use a lot of abstract verbiage to describe parts of arguments. We want to not only learn how to read this type of language but also to start to think more in terms of this language. When you're using abstract language, you're speaking about general forms / archetypes, and the magic of getting better at standardized tests is getting better at recognizing the general forms / archetypes that show up a lot on that test.\n\nWe should get used to the most typical types of reasoning moves:\n- **Analogies** / Distinctions\n- Suggesting / Ruling out **Alternatives**\n- **Faulty Implications** of a Claim or Plan","right_md":"Length --  **10 questions, 15 mins** (1.5x = 23, 2x = 30)****\nTypes -- **Role** , **Method** , and **Main Conclusion**\nTests -- **135, 140, and 123**\nLevels -- **1 - 4**\n\n- Whenever you feel pretty low confidence or ambivalent about your guess, hit the FLAG / BOOKMARK icon to the left of the question stem. We want to remember later that we weren't fully convinced of our answer, so that we can think about why not.\n\n- As you review the questions, after you've seen the explanation, type up any takeaways you want remember in the NOTEPAD window (a movable, resizable popup you get by clicking on the icon in the lower left of the page)."}$gd$::jsonb,$gd${"qtypes":["Role","Method","Main Conclusion"],"features":["Describing Arguments"],"reasoning_types":[],"difficulty_min":null,"difficulty_max":null,"count":10}$gd$::jsonb,$gd$lsatlab-studyplan$gd$,true)
ON CONFLICT (slug) DO UPDATE SET instruction_card=EXCLUDED.instruction_card, bank_filter=EXCLUDED.bank_filter, question_count=EXCLUDED.question_count, estimated_minutes=EXCLUDED.estimated_minutes, difficulty_min=EXCLUDED.difficulty_min, difficulty_max=EXCLUDED.difficulty_max, updated_at=now();

